# Trivy filesystem (Python dependency) baseline.
#
# Contract: explicit, dated and reviewable, exactly like
# `trivy-config.trivyignore`. Each entry carries `# expiry: YYYY-MM-DD` and
# `scripts/check_baselines_expiry.py` fails the build once past that date.
# Do NOT use trivy's own `expiredAt:` field — it is accepted and silently not
# honoured (measured at 0.71.0; see ADR-046).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
# ---------------------------
# The `Trivy filesystem scan` step was blocking (`exit-code: 1`) and had never
# examined a single Python dependency. Its own log said so on every run:
#
#     INFO  Number of language-specific files  num=0
#     WARN  [report] Supported files for scanner(s) not found.  scanners=[vuln]
#
# Trivy reads a requirements file only when versions are pinned exactly.
# This repository mandates `~=` — deliberately: numpy 2.x silently corrupts
# joblib models — and ships no lock file, so trivy had nothing to read.
# `scripts/resolve_python_dependencies.py` now resolves each requirements file
# with pip and hands trivy the concrete versions.
#
# Turning the gate on surfaced 24 fixable CRITICAL/HIGH findings that had been
# there all along. They are accepted HERE, with a date, rather than left
# invisible — and every NEW finding blocks immediately, which is the whole
# point of switching a dormant gate on instead of deleting it.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION, stated rather than hidden: the plain ignore format
# suppresses a vulnerability id REPO-WIDE. If a second package were ever
# affected by the same CVE, this would silence it there too. Accepted for the
# same reason as the config baseline: trivy's YAML format supports `paths:`
# scoping but silently ignores `expiredAt:`, and one parser over one format is
# worth more than per-path precision.
#
# NONE of these is fixable within its declared pin range — every fix requires a
# MAJOR version bump, which is a product decision with its own test run, not a
# side effect of switching on a scanner. `.github/dependabot.yml` now watches
# pip, so those upgrades arrive as their own PRs with CI attached.


# --- mlflow — CLOSED 2026-09-10, migrated to `~= 3.16.0` (ADR-047) ------
#
# Twenty entries lived here, {'CRITICAL': 7, 'HIGH': 13}, the largest cluster
# by far. All twenty are gone: mlflow 3.16.0 carries no fixable CRITICAL/HIGH
# findings at this gate's own threshold, measured rather than assumed.
#
# The review question this baseline asked was "has the mlflow 3.x migration ADR
# been written?" It had been (ADR-047), and its conclusion was wrong: it
# verified `_log_to_mlflow` in isolation and reported the model round-trip
# intact, while the SHIPPED pipeline fails on 3.x because skops refuses
# `sklearn.compose._column_transformer._RemainderColsList` from the
# ColumnTransformer's remainder. Running the whole pipeline is what found that.
# Fixed by deriving the trusted types from the fitted pipeline; all three load
# paths — sklearn, pyfunc, and the `@champion` alias — reproduce the joblib
# artefact's predictions exactly.
#
# Nothing is suppressed here now. If mlflow findings reappear, they block.

# --- starlette — transitive via `fastapi ~= 0.115.0` -------------
#
# 3 findings ({'HIGH': 3}), installed 0.46.2.
# Not directly pinned. Reaching starlette >= 0.49.1 requires a fastapi bump
# outside the declared compatible-release range. Two of the three are
# StaticFiles/Range-header issues on surfaces this template does not mount
# (no StaticFiles, no Range handling); the third is a form-parsing DoS that
# does apply to `/predict` under a hostile client.
#
# Review question: does the pinned fastapi range now reach a fixed
# starlette? That is a one-line check and the cheapest of the three to close.
#
# expiry: 2026-12-08  reason: starlette 0.46.2 -> needs 0.49.1 (major bump); HIGH
CVE-2025-62727
# expiry: 2026-12-08  reason: starlette 0.46.2 -> needs 1.1.0 (major bump); HIGH
CVE-2026-48818
# expiry: 2026-12-08  reason: starlette 0.46.2 -> needs 1.3.1 (major bump); HIGH
CVE-2026-54283

# --- pyarrow — pinned `~= 18.0.0` for the parquet prediction log (ADR-006)
#
# 1 findings ({'HIGH': 1}), installed 18.0.0.
# A use-after-free DoS in the Arrow C++ layer. The template's exposure is the
# prediction logger writing parquet it produced itself, not parsing
# attacker-supplied Arrow data, which is the vector the advisory describes.
#
# Review question: does anything now READ third-party parquet? If so this
# acceptance is void and the bump becomes urgent.
#
# expiry: 2026-12-08  reason: pyarrow 18.0.0 -> needs 23.0.1 (major bump); HIGH
CVE-2026-25087
